course: dictionary
lesson: 10876
title: One word, two jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Viking is a word you will meet in history lessons, and it can do two jobs. As a noun it names people: a Viking, or several Vikings. As an adjective it describes a thing: a Viking longship is a ship of the Vikings. The words around it show you which job it is doing.
explain: «Vikings led by Leif Erikson reached North America about a thousand years ago.» Here Vikings names the people who did the reaching, so it is a noun. In «a Viking longship», Viking comes before the noun longship and tells you what kind of ship it is, so it is an adjective.
explain: Context also helps you understand what the word means. The entry says Vikings were seafaring people who sailed far, so you can build the meaning from the clues. Then check: swap your own meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Viking is a noun for people: a Viking, Vikings.
fact: Viking is also an adjective, meaning of or about the Vikings: a Viking longship.
fact: The Vikings were seafaring people from Scandinavia, now Norway, Denmark and Sweden.
fact: «Vikings wrote with runes», letters of straight lines cut into wood and stone.
fact: Some everyday English words, such as «sky» and «egg», came into English from Old Norse, the Vikings' language.
exercise: «Vikings led by Leif Erikson reached North America about a thousand years ago.» In this sentence, what kind of word is Vikings? | A question word | A verb, an action word | *A noun, the name of people | An adjective, describing a thing
exercise: «A Viking longship» is a ship. What does Viking tell you about it? | It is made of gold | It is very small | *It belonged to the Vikings | It sails only in the sky
exercise: «Runes» are letters of straight lines. Where were they cut? | Onto a song | *Into wood and stone | Under the sea | Into the sky
test: «The horned helmets came from costumes for an opera in 1876.» Did Vikings really wear horned helmets in battle? | Yes, all of them did | Yes, but only the kings did | *No, the horns came from costumes made for an opera in 1876
test: Which word came into English from the Vikings' language? | *egg | wizard | castle | tomato
exam: What is the difference between Viking as a noun and Viking as an adjective? | As a noun it names a ship; as an adjective it names a sea | As a noun it means a long sea journey; as an adjective it means cold | *As a noun it names people; as an adjective it describes a thing, like a Viking longship | They are two different words with two different spellings
